==Old Norse==

===Etymology 1===
From the same ultimate origin as {{term|sá|lang=non}} and {{term|þat|lang=non}}. Related to Old English {{term|se|lang=ang}}. Cognate with Old English {{term|þes|lang=ang}}, Old High German {{term|dese|lang=goh}}, English {{term|this|lang=en}} and German {{term|diese|lang=de}}.

====Pronoun====
{{head|non|demonstrative pronoun|neuter|þetta}}

# {{demonstrative|lang=is}} [[this]], [[that]] (referring to both persons and things)

=====Declension=====
{{non-decl-pron-sjá}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{proto|Germanic|sehwanan|to see|lang=non}}. Cognates: see there.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|non|séa}}

====Verb====
{{head|non|verb}}

# to [[see]]

=====Descendants=====
* Faroese: {{l|fo|sjá}}
* Icelandic: {{l|is|sjá}}
* Norwegian Nynorsk: {{l|nn|sjå}}
<!-- not sure if the descendants of "séa" should be listed here, too -->

